\title{Implication in English}
\date{2026-01-28T21:04:44Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{There are \em{lots} of ways to express conditionals in English besides just "if P then Q".  For example:
\ul{
  \li{if P, Q}
  \li{P implies Q}
  \li{Q if P}
  \li{P only if Q}
  \li{Q whenever P}
}
and others. If you're not sure, make a truth table!}

\p{Again, you need not memorize this list, but it's good to be aware of these.}
